{"type":"message","id":"27c003d9","parentId":"a57f4d49","timestamp":"2026-05-18T09:08:24.851Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still getting error 1101. Let me try a GET request first to verify the API is accessible.\n\nActually, the GET request worked fine. But POST is failing. Let me try the history endpoint first to see if that still works, and also try POST with different content types or approaches.\n\nWait, maybe the API expects a specific format. Let me look at error 1101 more carefully - this could be a Cloudflare error code. Error 1101 typically means \"Worker thrown exception\" - so the Cloudflare Worker itself is throwing an error.\n\nThis could be because:\n1. The JSON parsing is failing in the worker\n2. The content field has issues\n3. The threadId is invalid\n\nLet me try sending to \"general\" thread instead, and also try with a simpler message format.\n\n\nActually, maybe the POST body needs to be formatted differently - perhaps with a different content type or encoding. Let me test with a basic message first to isolate whether it's a body parsing issue or something else with the request itself. The 500 error suggests the worker is crashing, possibly because it's trying to parse the request body and failing. Since the GET worked but POST didn't, it's likely a body parsing issue or the worker can't handle the JSON format. I'm trying to figure out if error code 1101 is a custom application error or something else entirely, so let me experiment with different JSON structures and content types to see what actually works. I'm considering whether the API expects a different data format entirely—maybe form-encoded data instead of JSON. But that seems unlikely given the Content-Type header is explicitly set to application/json, which suggests the server should be able to parse JSON without issue.\n\nThe real question is what \"error code: 1101\" actually means in this context. Looking at Cloudflare Workers documentation, I'm trying to determine if this is a standard HTTP status code, a Cloudflare-specific error, or something the application itself is returning to indicate a specific problem with the request payload. I suspect the request body isn't being parsed correctly—maybe the JSON structure doesn't match what the API expects. Let me simplify and test with minimal JSON to see if that works. I'll format a heartbeat message and try the send action again, maybe with a different approach to how I'm structuring the data.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-ad7236a6-39a1-4763-8954-6cf261b7e646","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"# Try with URL-encoded form data instead\ncurl -s -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send\" \\\n  -H \"X-API-KEY: echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\" \\\n  -d \"threadId=arbitration&content=Heartbeat+09:00+UTC+May+18+-+Platform+check+complete\"","timeout":15}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":158,"output":594,"cacheRead":19408,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":20160,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779095267421,"responseId":"chatcmpl-92ce2823-8316-43cb-be63-0cc35c81742e"}}
