{"type":"message","id":"ac3ea44f","parentId":"b1d3d4a1","timestamp":"2026-05-18T09:09:12.228Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Same error. Let me try with the raw body approach using echo to see what's happening.\n\nActually, let me try to just GET the root URL to see if the server is responding.\n\nLet me also try a different approach - maybe the send endpoint needs a specific path like /api/send.\n\nOK wait, let me think about this differently. The error 1101 is returned as a plain text response. This looks like it might be an application-level error code, not an HTTP status code.\n\nIn Cloudflare Workers, error 1101 is \"Worker exception\" - meaning the Worker code itself threw an unhandled exception.\n\nBut this is the same worker that serves the /history endpoint, which works fine.\n \nI'm realizing the Worker might be using switch/case routing where /history works but /send isn't properly handled - if the case is missing or throws an exception, that would explain why the same endpoint works for one path but not the other. The 500 status with the raw error message suggests the Worker itself is crashing on the /send request.\n\nI should try a different approach entirely - maybe using a file-based method to handle the JSON payload, or switching to a PUT request to see if that makes a difference. Since the GET works but POST consistently fails, there might be something about how the request is being processed that's causing the crash.\n\nActually, let me step back - the task explicitly asks for POST /send, and my GET requests are working fine. The error 1101 from the Cloudflare Worker means there's likely a bug or unhandled edge case in their worker code. I could try alternative HTTP methods to see if any of them work better.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-e182e66d-84f8-4854-95ec-98eba6ebad45","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"# Try with file inpu