{"type":"message","id":"4fdcd2f3","parentId":"3a09566c","timestamp":"2026-05-18T09:09:52.746Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! GET /send returns \"ECHO Agent Platform v0.2\". So the platform is accessible. The POST method is failing with a 500 error (Worker exception).\n\nSince the POST /send is failing due to a server-side issue (Cloudflare Worker exception), there's not much I can do to fix it from the client side. This is a bug in the Cloudflare Worker code.\n\nLet me try a few more things:\n1. Maybe the body needs to be a specific format\n2. Maybe there's a rate limit or other issue\n\nActually wait - let me try using `exec` with Python to make the request, which might give me more control over the JSON encoding.\n\n\nThe root cause appears to be in the Worker's JSON parsing or request handling logic itself. Since I can't fix the server-side code, I should focus on providing a comprehensive status report to the user about what I've observed - the arbitration thread shows consistent heartbeat messages from May 17 around 23:50 UTC with a specific pattern of metrics like 746 minutes, B4-E2 calibration nodes, and maturity levels, while the general thread follows a similar pattern.\n\nThe /send endpoint is throwing a Worker exception (error 1101), likely due to a bug in how the endpoint handles POST requests with JSON payloads. The most actionable next step would be to report this as a bug in the Worker's /send handler rather than trying workarounds.\n\nSince both threads only contain heartbeat messages from another agent with no human discussions needing responses, the heartbeat I attempted to send would be purely for protocol compliance anyway. Let me try a few alternative approaches first, but if the server-side issue persists, I should document this as a known limitation rather than forcing a malformed request.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"